  lenses: [
    {id:"production", question:"How is knowledge produced?", title:"Methods, evidence, and warranted claims", source:"Lazar, Feng & Hochheiser; Olson & Kellogg", href:"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4939-0378-8", detail:"Begin with the contribution a study seeks to make, then inspect how its evidence and method warrant that claim. HCI's plurality is not a menu of interchangeable techniques. Different traditions produce different forms of knowledge and judge quality differently.", implications:["State the intended contribution before choosing a method.","Separate evidence collection from the logic used to warrant a claim.","Use mixed methods only when their different warrants can be related."]},
    {id:"cognition", question:"Where does cognition happen?", title:"From an internal process to embodied and distributed activity", source:"Turner, 2016", href:"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-42235-0", detail:"Turner follows a movement from classical cognition toward mediated, situated, embodied, distributed, extended, and enactive accounts. The practical question is where a study locates thinking: in an individual, in action, across people and artifacts, or in a coupled organism-environment relation.", implications:["Choose a unit of analysis that fits the account of cognition.","Treat artifacts and environments as possible parts of cognitive activity.","Do not use post-cognitive as a claim that cognition has disappeared."]},
    {id:"relation", question:"What is the human-technology relation?", title:"Control, augmentation, partnership, and responsibility", source:"Chignell et al., 2023", href:"https://doi.org/10.1145/3557891", detail:"Chignell and colleagues read HCI and human factors through partial disciplinary separation and possible reintegration. Their account directs attention to augmentation, inclusive design, trust, workload, safety, situation awareness, and the changing relation between human and artificial intelligence.", implications:["Ask whether technology replaces, supports, coordinates with, or reshapes human activity.","Keep safety, trust, inclusion, and workload visible in human-AI design.","Read this as one disciplinary history alongside other historical framings."]}
  ],
